Validated Falsification Architecture for Anomaly Identifiability, with a Bounded Simulation-Model Application

ouadi Maakoul and SI TEAM 

1. Abstract

Most formulations of the simulation hypothesis generate no computable predictions and are therefore not testable. This thesis makes two separable contributions. Methodologically, it develops and validates a simulation-based architecture for assessing whether an apparent physical anomaly is identifiable, robust to plausible systematic effects, and reproducible under prespecified procedures — with operating characteristics derived from the likelihood structure and then validated against the asymptotic reference, not merely asserted, before the architecture is applied to real data. Theoretically, it derives a Poisson binned-likelihood constraint on one mathematically specified lattice model (Beane, Davoudi & Savage 2014) using public cosmic-ray spectrum data, with an explicit energy-scale nuisance and a pre-declared “unconstrained” outcome, as a bounded application of the Track B branch.

The empirical testbed for the methodology is the contested spatial-dipole claim in the fine-structure constant. This is chosen because the controversy is already documented at the systematic level — the claimed dipole’s equator aligns closely with the North–South divide between the Keck and VLT sightlines (Cameron & Pettitt 2012), and wavelength-calibration distortions of ±200 m s⁻¹ per 1000 Å are sufficient to mimic important features of the signal (Whitmore & Murphy 2015). The testbed is therefore a known-hard case for a detector, which is what makes it a useful validation target.

The thesis does not assume the universe is simulated. It tests whether particular, specified models make observable predictions distinguishable from conventional alternatives, and it treats “the data cannot distinguish these explanations at the required sensitivity” as a valid scientific result.

2. Research question

Primary: Can a prespecified, simulation-validated architecture distinguish a genuine physical anomaly from plausible systematic effects at a declared sensitivity — and can it be made to fail informatively when the available data cannot support that discrimination?

Sub-questions:

Q1 (methodological, primary). What validation architecture — feasibility gating, independent development/calibration/held-out simulation splits, positive and negative controls, power computed from the profiled information at the claimed dipole direction, Monte Carlo precision targets tied to binomial confidence intervals — is required for an anomaly-classification framework to be mathematically auditable?

Q2 (empirical, Track A). Does the fine-structure dipole contribute information beyond a per-instrument artifact model when calibration distortions are modelled at the spectral level, and at what sensitivity can the data discriminate the two?

Q3 (theoretical, Track B). What Poisson binned-likelihood bound does the observed ultra-high-energy cosmic-ray spectrum place on a specified lattice model, robust to astrophysical propagation and energy-scale nuisance parameters, and what future sensitivity would improve it?

Q4 (transferability). Does the architecture generalise to a second anomaly class without re-tuning?

3. Contribution and novelty

3.1 What is already established (not claimed as novel)

The literature is more developed than v0.3 implied, and the proposal’s novelty must be stated against it accurately:

· Bayesian model comparison of competing explanations for the α-dipole, including systematic-error models, has been done. Cameron & Pettitt (2012) compared null, monopole, and monopole+dipole hypotheses under several parametric forms for the unexplained error term, explicitly motivated by the telescope-divide confound.
· Wavelength-calibration distortions have been quantified and their impact on Δα/α simulated. Whitmore & Murphy (2015) used archival calibration observations (“supercalibration”) to characterise long-range distortions and demonstrated that a model of these distortions reproduces important aspects of the published signal.
· Lattice-model constraints from cosmic-ray data have been derived. Beane, Davoudi & Savage (2014) obtained b⁻¹ ≳ 10¹¹ GeV from the observed high-energy cutoff of the cosmic-ray spectrum.
· Canonical correlation is an established identifiability diagnostic, not a novel tool; its relation to profile-likelihood identifiability in the Gaussian linear model is elementary and known.

3.2 The claimed contribution (integrated architecture with a likelihood-based foundation)

The novelty claim is the integration of five elements that have not, to current knowledge, been combined in a single prespecified protocol, together with a likelihood-based treatment of the inference problem that makes the operating characteristics derivable and then validated against the asymptotic reference:

1. A latent spectral-level calibration model entering the absorber-level likelihood as a structured nuisance term — not a post-hoc correction, and not a single per-instrument offset.
2. A held-out validation layer: development / calibration / held-out simulation split so that the operating characteristics (power, FPR, discrimination threshold) are estimated on data the pipeline was not tuned on.
3. A prespecified decision rule that can return “unidentifiable” or “underpowered” as first-class outcomes, with the feasibility gate (G0) running before unblinding, and with the decision regions defined as a partition rather than in prose.
4. Explicit Monte Carlo precision targets for the false-positive rate, stated in advance with a binomial-interval framework and a computational feasibility study.
5. A likelihood-based foundation section (revised in v0.6) stating the observation model in partitioned Fisher-information form, the non-central χ² power calculation at the claimed dipole direction, the identification of the actual boundary-null sources (per-instrument scatter at zero; informative priors), and the calibration of the Bayes factor as a classifier ( §5.10 ).

This combination — and specifically the likelihood-based derivation of the power calculation together with the empirical validation of the χ² reference against Monte Carlo — is the methodological contribution. The empirical result on the α-dipole is an application, not the novelty.

3.3 Novelty gate (mandatory, Year 1)

Before the claim in §3.2 is frozen into any publication, a systematic literature review must be completed and documented: published papers, preprints (arXiv: astro-ph, physics.data-an, stat.ME), and methods literature on anomaly detection, identifiability, and Bayesian model validation. The review must specifically check whether (i) latent spectral-level calibration models have been used as nuisance terms in Δα/α likelihoods, (ii) held-out simulation validation has been applied to quasar-absorption model comparison, (iii) any prespecified “unidentifiable” decision rule exists in the astronomical spectroscopy literature, and (iv) non-central χ² power calculations with profiled nuisances have been used in this context. The review outcome may narrow the novelty claim; it will not be used to widen it.

4. Background

4.1 The simulation literature

Most simulation arguments (Bostrom 2003 and the ancestor-simulation tradition) are modal and non-predictive. A minority is constructive. Beane, Davoudi & Savage (2014) showed that a numerical universe on a cubic lattice with Wilson-type discretisation imposes an ultraviolet cutoff, constraining the lattice spacing via the observed ultra-high-energy cosmic-ray spectrum, and predicts rotational-symmetry-breaking signatures. This is the class the thesis treats as falsifiable — specified lattice models with calculable consequences, not “the Matrix.”

The thesis adopts an evidence firewall: a general anomaly-detection result counts as evidence for a simulation model only if (a) the model predicted that result in advance and (b) the prediction is more probable under the model than under the best available conventional alternative. Prior prediction alone is insufficient; diagnosticity is required.

4.2 The α-dipole controversy — audited state of play

The claim and its critiques, stated at the level of specificity the audit will confirm:

· Webb et al. (2011) reported a spatial dipole in Δα/α at approximately 4σ from combined Keck/HIRES and VLT/UVES quasar absorption spectra.
· King et al. (2012) extended the VLT/UVES sample.
· Cameron & Pettitt (2012), reanalysing the same data, found weak support for a skeptical interpretation in which the apparent dipole is driven by systematic errors of opposing sign between the two telescopes, and identified the close alignment of the dipole equator with the Keck/VLT North–South sightline divide as the primary reason for skepticism.
· Whitmore & Murphy (2015) found wavelength-calibration distortions to be “ubiquitous and substantial,” with long-range distortions of ±200 m s⁻¹ per 1000 Å, and showed that a model of these distortions reproduces important aspects of the VLT-UVES results and partially explains the HIRES results.
· Wilczynska et al. (2020) reported four direct measurements from a single quasar (J1120+0641) with X-shooter, with a weighted mean consistent with no variation, and separately combined those with prior measurements to estimate an updated dipole.

Consequence for the data plan: the four X-shooter measurements are summary measurements at one sky location, not per-absorber rows, and must not be pooled with the per-absorber samples as independent observations. The provenance and overlap of any combined sample must be established row-by-row in the audit. All numerical values in this paragraph are provisional until the §5.1 audit.

5. Methodology

5.1 Stage 0 — Data and identifiability feasibility (G0, runs first)

Runs before any control or unblinding, and before S4 becomes a pass/fail gate.

Data audit (hard gate).

· Verify recoverability of per-absorber Δα/α values, uncertainties, instrument provenance, redshift, transition sets, and the relevant calibration information.
· Document overlap between Webb 2011, King 2012, Wilczynska 2020, and any reused absorbers; define the row-level unit of analysis.
· Establish explicitly that Wilczynska’s four X-shooter measurements are not entered as independent per-absorber rows.
· Verify every secondary-dataset citation against its primary paper.

Identifiability and power analysis (likelihood-based, revised in v0.6).

Construct the design matrix for (d, \delta_{\text{instr}}) under the Gaussian linear model with known variances. Partition the Fisher information matrix:

\mathcal{I} = \begin{pmatrix} \mathcal{I}_{DD} & \mathcal{I}_{D\Delta} \\ \mathcal{I}_{\Delta D} & \mathcal{I}_{\Delta\Delta} \end{pmatrix}.

The Schur complement

S = \mathcal{I}_{DD} - \mathcal{I}_{D\Delta}\,\mathcal{I}_{\Delta\Delta}^{-1}\,\mathcal{I}_{\Delta D}

is the information about d remaining after profiling over \delta_{\text{instr}}. Power is a non-central \chi^2_3 with non-centrality \lambda = d^\top S d, evaluated at the claimed dipole direction and the audited amplitude A_{\text{claim}}^{\text{aud}}. This is the primary power calculation. It depends on the direction of the claimed dipole relative to the near-null directions of S, not on a single scalar canonical correlation.

The squared canonical correlations \rho_k^2 — the eigenvalues of \mathcal{I}_{DD}^{-1/2}\mathcal{I}_{D\Delta}\mathcal{I}_{\Delta\Delta}^{-1}\mathcal{I}_{\Delta D}\mathcal{I}_{DD}^{-1/2} — are reported as a diagnostic spectrum: they identify which directions in dipole space are poorly constrained. A near-one \rho_{\max} along a direction far from the claimed dipole costs little power at the claimed direction; a moderate \rho_k along the claimed direction costs power directly. The reported diagnostic is therefore the projection of the claimed dipole direction onto the eigenvector(s) associated with the smallest eigenvalues of S, not \rho_{\max} alone.

Formal power gate (revised in v0.6). The feasibility criterion is \lambda(A_{\text{claim}}^{\text{aud}}, \hat{d}_{\text{claim}}) \ge \lambda_{\min}, where \lambda_{\min} is the non-centrality required for the profile-likelihood test to attain power 1-\beta at level \alpha (e.g., for \alpha = 1.35\times 10^{-3}, 1-\beta = 0.80, and a non-central \chi^2_3, \lambda_{\min} is a fixed number computable from the non-central χ² distribution). This replaces the v0.5 criterion “\rho_{\max} > \rho^*” as the decision rule. The \{\rho_k\} spectrum is retained as a diagnostic and as an input to the reported sensitivity figure, not as a hard threshold.

Feasibility demonstration. Show by simulation that a dipole at the claimed direction and audited amplitude is distinguishable from plausible instrument artifacts at the required power, using the design-conditional parametric bootstrap ( §5.5 ). The Gaussian non-central χ² calculation provides the analytic reference; the Monte Carlo quantifies departures from it.

Failure condition: if \lambda < \lambda_{\min} at the claimed direction and amplitude, or the required data are not recoverable, stop before unblinding and report infeasible / underpowered. This is a valid thesis outcome.

5.2 Observation model

\Delta\alpha/\alpha_i = d\cdot\hat{n}_i + B + \delta_{\text{instr}(i)} + \varepsilon_i.

· d \in \mathbb{R}^3, linear parameterisation, no sky-grid search. d=0 is an interior point of \mathbb{R}^3 (revised in v0.6): there is no amplitude boundary and no unidentified-direction (Davies) problem for d itself. The direction is reported as d/|d| with a confidence region when |d| is bounded away from zero; when the confidence region for d includes the origin, the direction is not reported as a point estimate.
· \delta_{\text{ref}} = 0 for a prespecified reference instrument; statistics are reference-invariant, individual \delta values are not physical parameters.
· \varepsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma_i^2 + \sigma_{\text{int,instr}(i)}^2), per-instrument intrinsic scatter with a hierarchical prior; common-scatter model run as a sensitivity check.

Latent spectral-level calibration model. Because the documented distortions are wavelength-dependent and can mimic the signal, the observation model distinguishes:

· nuisance effects estimated directly from spectral data (calibration distortions, continuum placement);
· effects propagated into each absorber’s reported uncertainty (isotope abundances, transition-set choice);
· effects represented as additional correlated errors (velocity structure, selection correlated with sky position).

Which effects sit in which category is fixed at freeze. A single per-instrument constant offset is explicitly not claimed to represent the wavelength-dependent distortions documented by Whitmore & Murphy; the protocol models those distortions as a latent term.

5.3 Four-model comparison, the χ² reference, and where boundary effects actually arise

Model Structure
M0 B only
M1 B + d\cdot\hat{n}
M2 B + \delta_{\text{instr}}
M3 B + d\cdot\hat{n} + \delta_{\text{instr}}

The primary statistic is the profile likelihood ratio M_3 vs M_2 — does a dipole contribute information beyond instrument offsets? This targets the telescope-divide confound identified by Cameron & Pettitt.

Three variants: (a) \delta_{\text{instr}} \equiv 0 (historical analysis class); (b) \delta_{\text{instr}} free with archive-calibration prior (decision variant); (c) \delta_{\text{instr}} free with weak prior (robustness).

Asymptotic reference and the honest justification for Monte Carlo calibration (rewritten in v0.6). Under the linear parametrization d \in \mathbb{R}^3 with known variances, d=0 is an interior point of the parameter space. For M3 vs M2, the profiled likelihood ratio is therefore exactly \chi^2_3 under the null (non-central \chi^2_3 under alternatives with non-centrality \lambda = d^\top S d). The null distribution does not depend on the design; non-orthogonality between \delta and d changes \lambda (the power) and not the null. The boundary-null / chi-bar-squared apparatus of Chernoff (1954), Self & Liang (1987), and Shapiro (1985) does not apply to d. Citing it for d would be a mathematical error.

Boundary effects do arise elsewhere in this model, and they are the honest justification for Monte Carlo calibration:

1. The per-instrument intrinsic scatter \sigma_{\text{int}} \ge 0 sits on a boundary at zero. If the true scatter is zero or near zero, the null distribution of the LRT statistic for the scatter component is a chi-bar-squared mixture, and the fitted scatter can bias the dipole test through the variance model.
2. Small samples and the informative prior in variant (b) can distort the tail of the dipole statistic relative to the χ²₃ reference.
3. The Gaussian known-variance idealisation is an approximation; the actual uncertainties are estimated, and the hierarchical scatter model introduces additional finite-sample effects.

The Monte Carlo calibration is therefore a validation of χ²₃ adequacy under these realistic complications, not a mathematical necessity arising from a boundary in d. The Year-1 deliverable is to quantify departures from χ²₃ — comparing the Monte Carlo null quantiles to the χ²₃ quantiles as a function of sample size, \sigma_{\text{int}}, and prior informativeness — and to report the correction (if any) used in the decision rule. If the departures are negligible at the realised design, the χ²₃ reference is retained and the Monte Carlo is reported as a confirmation; if not, the empirical null is used and the χ²₃ tail is reported alongside as a diagnostic of miscalibration.

5.4 Monte Carlo precision target: a binomial-interval framework

The false-positive target is \alpha = 1.35\times 10^{-3}.

The v0.4 rule-of-three calculation — N > 3/\alpha \approx 2{,}223 for a zero-failure 95% upper bound — is correct as a special case, but it is not a general precision framework. The protocol fixes the framework in advance:

· Interval method: Clopper–Pearson exact binomial confidence interval (conservative coverage, no reliance on asymptotic normality).
· Target half-width: the protocol pre-specifies a target half-width h for the 95% interval on the true FPR, per variant. The required N is the smallest integer such that the Clopper–Pearson interval width at the anticipated FPR is at most 2h.
· Anticipated FPR: set to \alpha for the null-calibration simulations and to the simulated power for the positive-control simulations. If the anticipated FPR is near 10^{-3} and h = 5\times 10^{-4}, the required N is on the order of 10^4–10^5 per variant. The computational feasibility study ( §10 , Y1) produces this number and justifies the compute trade-off.
· Conditioning: the null simulations use the estimated nuisance parameters (parametric bootstrap), so the resulting FPR is conditional on the realised nuisance estimates, not marginal. This is stated as a limitation: it calibrates the pipeline under the fitted null, not under a prior for the nuisance parameters. A prior-predictive calibration is run as a sensitivity check if compute permits.

The proposal does not promise a validated false-positive rate merely because the pipeline has run thousands of times. The number of runs is derived from the precision target, and the precision target is stated before freeze.

5.5 Controls S1–S4 (S3 definition corrected)

Frozen and hashed before unblinding; pass criteria and target rates (default 80% power, FPR \le \alpha) written down in advance.

· S1 — global permutation: gross-pipeline check.
· S2 — within-instrument permutation: diagnostic only. Permutation disrupts the uncertainty, redshift, and selection relationships. Primary false-positive calibration comes from the parametric bootstrap preserving the design matrix, uncertainty structure, and instrument–sky correlation.
· S3 — synthetic H₁′ data with instrument offsets drawn from the archive-calibration prior, real sky distribution, real sample sizes: variants (b)/(c) must not return “survives” at rate \le \alpha, and must exhibit power on T_{\text{off}}. S3 is not required to return the “not robust” label, because “not robust” in §5.8 additionally requires T_{\text{hist}} to be significant, which depends on the synthetic injection and is not the quantity S3 is designed to test.
· S4 — positive control. Injection at A_{\text{claim}}^{\text{aud}} along the claimed direction; variant (b) must recover and classify “survives” at target rate. Also run on an amplitude grid and on resampled instrument–sky configurations to derive the empirical distribution of \lambda and the reported sensitivity curve.

Sequencing: G0 runs first. If the power gate \lambda < \lambda_{\min} fails, the study is pre-declared underpowered; S4 at that amplitude does not become a pass/fail gate.

Framing of S3: S3 is a stress test of the classifier under a specified alternative data-generating process. It is not a calibration of the FPR under model misspecification. The gap between the S3 DGP and the real-data DGP is stated explicitly, and the held-out FPR is not over-interpreted as evidence that the fitted null model is correct.

5.6 Held-out validation (three-tier split) with a formal tolerance

To eliminate tuning circularity:

1. Development set — pipeline construction.
2. Calibration set — power curves and sensitivity estimates.
3. Freeze and hash.
4. Held-out validation set — final performance.

Formal tolerance. The held-out FPR estimate \hat{\alpha}_{\text{held}} must satisfy a pre-specified binomial consistency criterion with \alpha: the 95% Clopper–Pearson interval for the true FPR computed from the held-out sample must contain \alpha. The held-out sample size is chosen in advance so that a factor-of-two inflation of the true FPR (from \alpha to 2\alpha) would be detected with at least 80% probability. The uncertainty around the power curve is reported, not point estimates.

Retuning protection. Development, calibration, and held-out simulations are separated. The permitted correction process is defined; the history of failed pipeline versions is retained and reported. Unblinding occurs once, after freeze; post-unblinding changes are logged deviations.

5.7 Bayesian layer, calibrated as a classifier

· Dipole prior: |d| \sim half-normal, direction uniform. Two prior scales are specified (corrected in v0.6): (i) the decision scale, matched to A_{\text{claim}}^{\text{aud}}, used in the decision rule; (ii) the diagnostic scale, broad and amplitude-agnostic, reported as a sensitivity check but not used for the decision. The decision rule anchors on the decision scale only; the diagnostic scale is explicitly not co-primary.
· Bayes factor rule anchored to the decision scale. Rationale: for nested comparisons, diffuse priors can favour the simpler model even under a real signal (Lindley’s paradox). The full prior-scale grid is reported as a diagnostic.
· Mathematical qualification: the anchored Bayes factor is a decision-theoretic classifier, not a prior-neutral evidence measure. Its operating characteristics (power, FPR) depend on the prior scale and on the true amplitude. It is therefore calibrated by the same held-out simulation machinery used for the frequentist statistic: the Bayes factor threshold is chosen on the calibration set to achieve the target FPR, and its power is measured on the held-out set. The Bayes factor is never presented as an unbiased summary of evidence independent of the prior.
· Posterior predictive checks assess the distribution of discrepancy statistics under the fitted null — FPR calibration, residual structure, coverage. Simulation-based calibration (SBC) (Talts et al. 2018) is used to validate the Bayesian computation, not to validate the scientific model.

5.8 Decision rule as a formal partition

Let \mathcal{D} = (G_0, \lambda, \{\rho_k\}, T_{\text{key}}, BF, T_{\text{hist}}, T_{\text{off}}) be the diagnostic vector. The decision regions are a partition of \mathcal{D} with boundaries fixed by the simulation study before freeze:

· G_0 fails, or data unrecoverable \;\to\; \mathcal{R}_{\text{infeasible}}.
· G_0 passes but \lambda < \lambda_{\min}, or S3/S4 discrimination below target \;\to\; \mathcal{R}_{\text{underpowered}}, regardless of p-values. Precedence is explicit: a dataset that is both underpowered and nominally significant is classified as underpowered, because the nominal significance is not interpretable at the declared sensitivity. The \{\rho_k\} spectrum is reported alongside, but does not by itself trigger this region.
· Variants (b) and (c) classify differently \;\to\; \mathcal{R}_{\text{prior-sensitive}}.
· T_{\text{key}} p < \alpha and BF concordant at the decision scale \;\to\; \mathcal{R}_{\text{survives}} (licenses Objective 2 only; does not establish new physics).
· T_{\text{key}} p \ge \alpha, T_{\text{hist}} significant, T_{\text{off}} significant \;\to\; \mathcal{R}_{\text{not-robust}} (consistent with instrument artifact).
· T_{\text{key}} p \ge \alpha, T_{\text{hist}} not significant \;\to\; \mathcal{R}_{\text{hist-null}} (consistent with an absent signal or pipeline divergence from the historical specification; both reported).
· All remaining discordant combinations \;\to\; \mathcal{R}_{\text{discordant}}.

The partition is accompanied by a region diagram in Ch. 4, so that the boundary between “underpowered” and “discordant” is not left to judgment.

5.9 Reporting: labels are not results

Every classification is accompanied by the underlying quantities:

Claim Reported evidence
A specified dipole model improves the fit beyond the artifact model Effect estimate, uncertainty interval, profile likelihood confidence region, BF at decision scale
The historical estimate is reproduced T_{\text{hist}} with its own effect estimate and interval
The data discriminate between these explanations at the prespecified sensitivity \lambda, power curve, \{\rho_k\} spectrum, S3/S4 discrimination rates

“Not reproduced” is never reported without all three. Failure to reproduce the historical significance threshold can arise from a changed dataset, a changed uncertainty model, lower power, or a different specification — and the report must say which.

5.10 Mathematical foundations of the architecture (rewritten in v0.6)

This subsection is the mathematical core of the methodological contribution and sits in Ch. 4. It states:

1. The observation model in partitioned Fisher-information form, with S = \mathcal{I}_{DD} - \mathcal{I}_{D\Delta}\mathcal{I}_{\Delta\Delta}^{-1}\mathcal{I}_{\Delta D} as the profiled information about d, and the non-central \chi^2_3 power calculation \lambda = d^\top S d at the claimed dipole direction.
2. The \chi^2_3 null reference for M3 vs M2 under the interior-point linear parametrization, with the explicit statement that Chernoff/Self–Liang boundary asymptotics do not apply to d, and the identification of the actual boundary-null sources (\sigma_{\text{int}} \ge 0, informative priors, finite-sample effects) that motivate Monte Carlo calibration as a validation of \chi^2_3 adequacy.
3. The canonical-correlation spectrum \{\rho_k\} as a diagnostic of the directions in which the profiled information is deficient, with the projection of the claimed dipole direction onto the small-eigenvalue eigenvectors reported alongside.
4. The binomial precision framework for the FPR target, with the Clopper–Pearson interval and the required N derived from the target half-width.
5. The Bayes-factor calibration as a classifier with its own null distribution and power curve, not as a prior-neutral evidence measure.
6. The decision-region partition with boundaries fixed by simulation, and the explicit precedence of the power gate over nominal significance.

6. Track B — bounded lattice-model constraint with a Poisson binned likelihood

Scope: one specified lattice model (Beane, Davoudi & Savage 2014), one principal observable (the ultra-high-energy cosmic-ray spectrum and its high-energy cutoff), one reproducible constraint calculation.

Likelihood specification (revised in v0.6). Public UHECR data are predominantly binned spectra, not event-level energies. The v0.5 event-level product \prod_i f(E_i) is therefore replaced by a Poisson binned likelihood:

\mathcal{L}(b^{-1}, \boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{prop}}, \eta) = \prod_{j} \frac{\mu_j(b^{-1}, \boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{prop}}, \eta)^{n_j}\, e^{-\mu_j(b^{-1}, \boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{prop}}, \eta)}}{n_j!},

where n_j is the observed count in energy bin j, \mu_j is the predicted count under the lattice-cutoff hypothesis, \boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{prop}} are astrophysical propagation nuisance parameters (source composition, maximum acceleration energy, photodisintegration and photopion losses, magnetic deflections), and \eta is an energy-scale nuisance parameter (a multiplicative shift of the reconstructed energy scale, constrained by its own prior or Gaussian likelihood).

The constraint on b^{-1} is a profile-likelihood bound obtained by profiling over (\boldsymbol{\theta}_{\text{prop}}, \eta) and taking the appropriate confidence level:

b^{-1} \ge b^{-1}_{\text{profiled}}(1-\gamma).

Pre-declared “unconstrained” outcome (new in v0.6). A lattice cutoff degenerate with the source maximum acceleration energy, or with a propagation cutoff, may leave the profiled bound vacuous. If the profile-likelihood confidence set for b^{-1} extends to the parameter-space boundary or to a pre-specified numerical upper limit b^{-1}_{\max}, the result is reported as “unconstrained at the current sensitivity” — a valid and informative outcome, not a failure. The conditions under which this outcome is declared are specified before fitting.

Deliverables:

· A restatement of the model’s assumptions and predicted signature (UV cutoff, rotational-symmetry breaking in the highest-energy cosmic-ray distribution).
· A Poisson binned-likelihood profile constraint on the lattice spacing from public spectrum data, with the current bound (b^{-1} \gtrsim 10^{11} GeV per Beane et al.) reproduced or refined, and with the nuisance-robustness of the bound stated.
· A statement of the future sensitivity required to distinguish the model from conventional physics, expressed as a target for a specified next-generation instrument.

Explicitly out of scope: a taxonomy of all conceivable simulation mechanisms. Chapter 2 provides only the minimal model taxonomy needed to justify selecting this one.

