11. Supervisor fit and framing

The thesis is framed so that a supervisor is not asked to endorse the simulation hypothesis. It is framed as a methodological contribution (a validated, simulation-based framework with likelihood-based power calculations and empirical validation of the χ² reference), a bounded empirical test, and a separate theoretical application (one lattice model, one observable, one Poisson binned-likelihood constraint).

The required supervisory expertise is astronomical spectroscopy (wavelength calibration, quasar absorption) and statistical model validation (simulation-based calibration, held-out evaluation, likelihood asymptotics). Neither expertise requires a prior interest in simulation arguments.

12. What the thesis does not claim

· That the universe is simulated.
· That a Track A residual is evidence for Track B without prior comparative prediction.
· That Bayesian model comparison or calibration-systematics modelling is novel.
· That a validated pipeline has been demonstrated before its operating characteristics are measured on held-out data with a formal binomial consistency criterion.
· That the anchored Bayes factor is a prior-neutral evidence measure; it is a calibrated classifier.
· That boundary-null / chi-bar-squared asymptotics apply to the dipole parameter d; they do not, because d=0 is an interior point of \mathbb{R}^3. Boundary effects are claimed only for \sigma_{\text{int}} \ge 0 and for prior/finite-sample distortions of the tail.
· That “not reproduced” is a single claim rather than a family of distinguishable outcomes.


Appendices to v0.6 — Mathematical Details and Proofs

The following appendices are part of Ch. 4 (Appendices A–J) and Ch. 8 (Appendix I). They are written to be self-contained: a referee who reads only the appendices should be able to verify every claim made in the main text.

Notation throughout: y \in \mathbb{R}^n is the vector of per-absorber \Delta\alpha/\alpha_i; X_d \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times 3} has rows \hat n_i^\top; X_\Delta \in \mathbb{R}^{n\times k} is the block indicator for the k instrument offsets; X_B = \mathbf{1}_n; X_\eta = [X_\Delta, X_B]; \Sigma = \operatorname{diag}(\sigma_i^2 + \sigma_{\text{int}, \text{instr}(i)}^2); W = \Sigma^{-1}. Parameter vectors: \theta = (d, \eta) with \eta = (\delta, B). We treat W as known in Appendices A–D and return to its estimation in Appendix E.

---

Appendix A — Setup, Fisher information, and partitioned WLS

A.1 The observation model in matrix form

The v0.6 observation model is

y = X_d\, d + X_\eta\, \eta + \varepsilon, \qquad \varepsilon \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma).

Two structural facts are used repeatedly.

(F1) The null is interior. d \in \mathbb{R}^3 with d=0 an interior point of the parameter space. There is no inequality constraint on d, and no identifiability ambiguity of the type treated by Davies (1977, 1987). The only boundary in the model is \sigma_{\text{int}} \ge 0 (Appendix E).

(F2) The nuisance block contains the intercept. X_B = \mathbf{1}_n \in \operatorname{span}(X_\Delta) — adding a constant to every \delta_j is equivalent to shifting B. This is the source of the reference-invariance property proven in Appendix I.

A.2 Weighted least squares and the Fisher information

The log-likelihood (with \Sigma known) is

\ell(d, \eta) = -\tfrac12 (y - X_d d - X_\eta \eta)^\top W (y - X_d d - X_\eta \eta) + \text{const}.

The Fisher information for \theta = (d, \eta) is

\mathcal{I} = \begin{pmatrix} \mathcal{I}_{DD} & \mathcal{I}_{D\eta} \\ \mathcal{I}_{\eta D} & \mathcal{I}_{\eta\eta} \end{pmatrix},
\qquad
\mathcal{I}_{DD} = X_d^\top W X_d, \quad
\mathcal{I}_{D\eta} = X_d^\top W X_\eta, \quad
\mathcal{I}_{\eta\eta} = X_\eta^\top W X_\eta.

A.3 The profiled information

Define the Schur complement

\boxed{\;S \;=\; \mathcal{I}_{DD} - \mathcal{I}_{D\eta}\, \mathcal{I}_{\eta\eta}^{-1}\, \mathcal{I}_{\eta D}.\;}

S \in \mathbb{R}^{3\times 3} is symmetric positive semidefinite, and positive definite iff X_d is not W-collinear with X_\eta on any nonzero direction of d.

Lemma A.1 (Block inverse). Let \mathcal{I} be as above with \mathcal{I}_{\eta\eta} invertible. Then the (d,d)-block of \mathcal{I}^{-1} is S^{-1}, and the (d,\eta)-block of \mathcal{I}^{-1} is -\mathcal{I}_{DD}^{-1}\mathcal{I}_{D\eta}S^{-1} or, equivalently, -S^{-1}\mathcal{I}_{D\eta}\mathcal{I}_{\eta\eta}^{-1}.

Proof. Standard partitioned-matrix inversion; see Magnus & Neudecker (1999, §2.11). \square

The lemma is what identifies S as the profiled (or marginal) information about d — the inverse of the covariance of \hat d after profiling over \eta. This is the mathematical content of the statement in §5.1 that S is "the information about d remaining after profiling over \delta_{\text{instr}}."

---

Appendix B — Profiled weighted least squares (Frisch–Waugh–Lovell)

The weighted Frisch–Waugh–Lovell (FWL) theorem is the algebraic engine behind the analysis. It is stated and proved here so that the LRT decomposition in Appendix C is self-contained.

Theorem B.1 (Weighted FWL). Let P_\eta = X_\eta(X_\eta^\top W X_\eta)^{-1}X_\eta^\top W be the W-orthogonal projector onto \operatorname{span}(X_\eta), and let M_\eta = I_n - P_\eta. Then:

(i) The WLS estimator of d in the full model equals the WLS estimator of d in the partialled model:

\hat d = \big(X_d^\top M_\eta^\top W M_\eta X_d\big)^{-1} X_d^\top M_\eta^\top W M_\eta\, y.

(ii) The information matrix for d in this partialled regression is exactly S:

X_d^\top M_\eta^\top W M_\eta X_d = X_d^\top W M_\eta X_d = S.

(iii) Under the model, \hat d \sim \mathcal{N}(d,\, S^{-1}).

Proof.

(i) Both sides solve the same normal equations. The full normal equations are X^\top W X \hat\theta = X^\top W y with X = [X_d, X_\eta]. Partitioning, the d-block reads X_d^\top W X_d\, \hat d + X_d^\top W X_\eta\, \hat\eta = X_d^\top Wy. From the \eta-block, \hat\eta = (X_\eta^\top WX_\eta)^{-1}X_\eta^\top W(y - X_d \hat d). Substituting:

\big(X_d^\top WX_d - X_d^\top WX_\eta(X_\eta^\top WX_\eta)^{-1}X_\eta^\top WX_d\big)\hat d = \big(X_d^\top W - X_d^\top WX_\eta(X_\eta^\top WX_\eta)^{-1}X_\eta^\top W\big)y.

The left-hand matrix is S by construction. The right-hand matrix is X_d^\top M_\eta^\top W. Since M_\eta^\top W = WM_\eta (both equal W - WX_\eta(X_\eta^\top WX_\eta)^{-1}X_\eta^\top W by direct expansion and symmetry of W), the equation is S\hat d = X_d^\top M_\eta^\top W y.

For the right side of (i): M_\eta^\top W M_\eta = M_\eta^\top W because M_\eta is idempotent (M_\eta^2 = M_\eta) and W-symmetric (M_\eta^\top W M_\eta = WM_\eta). Hence the partialled regression normal equations are identical to S\hat d = X_d^\top WM_\eta y, which is the same as the full model. \checkmark

(ii) Same expansion: X_d^\top M_\eta^\top WM_\eta X_d = X_d^\top WX_d - X_d^\top WX_\eta(X_\eta^\top WX_\eta)^{-1}X_\eta^\top WX_d = S. \checkmark

(iii) \hat d is linear in y, hence Gaussian. Its mean is S^{-1}X_d^\top WM_\eta\, E[y] = S^{-1}X_d^\top WM_\eta(X_d d + X_\eta \eta) = S^{-1}S\, d + 0 = d (the X_\eta\eta term is annihilated because M_\eta X_\eta = 0 by construction). Its covariance is

\operatorname{Var}(\hat d) = S^{-1}X_d^\top WM_\eta \cdot \Sigma \cdot M_\eta^\top W X_d S^{-1}.

Using \Sigma = W^{-1} and M_\eta^\top WM_\eta = WM_\eta:

\operatorname{Var}(\hat d) = S^{-1}X_d^\top WM_\eta X_d S^{-1} = S^{-1}S S^{-1} = S^{-1}. \qquad \square

Corollary B.2 (Direction of \hat d). Under \hat d \sim \mathcal{N}(d, S^{-1}), the Mahalanobis norm \|\hat d\|_S := (\hat d^\top S \hat d)^{1/2} is a natural test statistic. Its null distribution is computed in Appendix C.

