Appendix C — Exact null distribution of the dipole LRT

Theorem C.1 (Exact \chi^2_3 null). Under the Gaussian linear model of Appendix A with W = \Sigma^{-1} known, the profile likelihood ratio for testing H_0: d = 0 in the model M_3 (with \delta free) against the nested model M_2 (with \delta free, d=0) satisfies

-2\log\Lambda_{32}(y) \;=\; \hat d^\top S\, \hat d,

and under H_0 this statistic has exactly a \chi^2_3 distribution. The null distribution does not depend on the design, on \eta, or on the estimated nuisance parameters.

Proof.

Step 1 — Likelihood ratio decomposition. Let \hat\theta = (\hat d, \hat\eta) be the MLE in M_3 and \hat\eta_0 the MLE of \eta in M_2. Then

-2\log\Lambda_{32} = (y - X_\eta\hat\eta_0)^\top W(y - X_\eta\hat\eta_0) - (y - X_d\hat d - X_\eta\hat\eta)^\top W(y - X_d\hat d - X_\eta\hat\eta).

Write P_\eta^\perp := M_\eta^\top W M_\eta = WM_\eta (Appendix B) for the W-residual operator onto M_2, and P_{d,\eta}^\perp for the corresponding operator onto M_3. The first term is y^\top P_\eta^\perp y, the second is y^\top P_{d,\eta}^\perp y. By the FWL-style block identity,

P_\eta^\perp - P_{d,\eta}^\perp = P_\eta^\perp X_d\, (X_d^\top P_\eta^\perp X_d)^{-1}\, X_d^\top P_\eta^\perp.

Right-multiplication by y and left-multiplication by y^\top gives

-2\log\Lambda_{32} \;=\; (X_d^\top P_\eta^\perp y)^\top\, S^{-1}\, (X_d^\top P_\eta^\perp y).

By the FWL theorem, X_d^\top P_\eta^\perp y = S \hat d. Therefore -2\log\Lambda_{32} = \hat d^\top S \hat d. \checkmark

Step 2 — Null distribution. By Theorem B.1(iii), under H_0 (d=0), \hat d \sim \mathcal{N}(0, S^{-1}). Since S is positive definite, there exists a symmetric positive-definite square root S^{1/2} with S^{1/2}S^{1/2} = S. Then

S^{1/2}\hat d \sim \mathcal{N}(0, S^{1/2}S^{-1}S^{1/2}) = \mathcal{N}(0, I_3).

Hence

\hat d^\top S \hat d = \|S^{1/2}\hat d\|^2 = Z_1^2 + Z_2^2 + Z_3^2, \quad Z_i \stackrel{\text{iid}}{\sim}\mathcal{N}(0,1),

which is exactly \chi^2_3. Since the distribution of S^{1/2}\hat d under the null is design-free, so is the null distribution of \hat d^\top S\hat d. \square

Remark C.2 (Why boundary and Davies asymptotics do not apply to d). Chernoff (1954), Self & Liang (1987), Shapiro (1985), and Davies (1977, 1987) treat settings where the null places the parameter on the boundary of the parameter space or where the parameter is unidentified under the null. Neither applies here:

· d=0 is an interior point of \mathbb{R}^3, so the regular chi-squared asymptotics are exact (not just asymptotic) in the known-W case.
· Every component of d is identified under the null — the design matrix X_d has rank 3 by construction, and S \succ 0 by the feasibility condition of §5.1.

The residual concern in v0.5 — the non-orthogonality between \delta and d — affects the non-centrality under H_1 (the power), not the null. This is proven in Appendix D. This is the mathematical error v0.5 made and v0.6 corrects.

