Appendix D — Non-central \chi^2_3 power and the canonical-correlation spectrum

Theorem D.1 (Exact power). Under the alternative d = d_\star, the LRT statistic T = \hat d^\top S \hat d has a non-central \chi^2_3 distribution with non-centrality

\boxed{\;\lambda(d_\star) \;=\; d_\star^\top S\, d_\star.\;}

Proof. Under H_1, \hat d \sim \mathcal{N}(d_\star, S^{-1}). Then S^{1/2}\hat d \sim \mathcal{N}(S^{1/2}d_\star, I_3), and

T = \|S^{1/2}\hat d\|^2 \sim \chi^2_3(\lambda), \quad \lambda = \|S^{1/2}d_\star\|^2 = d_\star^\top S d_\star. \qquad \square

Corollary D.2 (Power at the claimed dipole). The feasibility gate in §5.1 is \lambda(A_{\text{claim}}^{\text{aud}}, \hat d_{\text{claim}}) \ge \lambda_{\min}, where \lambda_{\min} is the non-centrality such that a non-central \chi^2_3(\lambda_{\min}) variable exceeds the (1-\alpha)-quantile of \chi^2_3 with probability 1-\beta. For \alpha = 1.35\times 10^{-3} and 1-\beta = 0.80, \lambda_{\min} is a fixed number computable from the non-central \chi^2 distribution (e.g., numerically, \lambda_{\min} \approx 20.5; see below).

Numerical remark. For \alpha = 1.35\times 10^{-3}, the \chi^2_3 critical value is q \approx 15.78. The non-central \chi^2_3(\lambda) power against q is monotone in \lambda; solving for 0.80 power gives \lambda_{\min} \approx 20.5. The exact value is computed by the non-central \chi^2 CDF and does not depend on the data — only on (\alpha, 1-\beta, \text{df}=3).

D.1 Canonical correlations and the spectral decomposition of S

Definition D.3 (Canonical correlations). Let

\Lambda \;=\; \mathcal{I}_{DD}^{-1/2}\,\mathcal{I}_{D\eta}\,\mathcal{I}_{\eta\eta}^{-1}\,\mathcal{I}_{\eta D}\,\mathcal{I}_{DD}^{-1/2} \;\in\; \mathbb{R}^{3\times 3}.

Then \Lambda is symmetric positive semidefinite with eigenvalues \rho_1^2 \ge \rho_2^2 \ge \rho_3^2 \ge 0 (necessarily \le 1, since \Lambda is a "sandwich" of positive semidefinite matrices bounded by the identity — see Magnus & Neudecker 1999, §12.22). These are the squared canonical correlations between the d-block and the \eta-block.

Proposition D.4 (Spectral relationship between S and \Lambda).

S \;=\; \mathcal{I}_{DD}^{1/2}\,(I_3 - \Lambda)\,\mathcal{I}_{DD}^{1/2}.

Proof. Direct expansion:

\mathcal{I}_{DD}^{1/2}(I - \Lambda)\mathcal{I}_{DD}^{1/2}
= \mathcal{I}_{DD} - \mathcal{I}_{DD}^{1/2}\Lambda\mathcal{I}_{DD}^{1/2}
= \mathcal{I}_{DD} - \mathcal{I}_{D\eta}\mathcal{I}_{\eta\eta}^{-1}\mathcal{I}_{\eta D} = S. \qquad \square

Corollary D.5 (Interpretation of \rho_k). Let v_k be the eigenvector of \Lambda associated with \rho_k^2. Then u_k := \mathcal{I}_{DD}^{-1/2}v_k is a direction in d-space with the following property:

· If \rho_k \to 1: the u_k-direction of d is nearly absorbed by the nuisance block; S u_k \approx 0.
· If \rho_k \to 0: the u_k-direction of d is untouched by the nuisance block; the profiled information in that direction equals \mathcal{I}_{DD}, no information is lost.

Proposition D.6 (Direction-dependent power). For a claimed dipole direction \hat u_{\text{claim}} = \hat d_{\text{claim}}/|\hat d_{\text{claim}}|, the non-centrality at amplitude A = |d_\star| is

\lambda(A, \hat u_{\text{claim}}) = A^2\, \hat u_{\text{claim}}^\top S\, \hat u_{\text{claim}}.

In the eigenbasis \{u_k\} of S, writing \hat u_{\text{claim}} = \sum_k c_k u_k with \sum_k c_k^2 = 1:

\lambda(A, \hat u_{\text{claim}}) = A^2 \sum_k c_k^2\, s_k,

where s_k are the eigenvalues of S (and s_k decreases as \rho_k^2 increases). Power is lost only to the extent that the claimed direction loads on eigenvectors with small s_k — i.e., on the directions where the nuisance block absorbs the dipole.

Consequence (the correction from v0.5 to v0.6). Power depends on the direction of d_\star, not on \rho_{\max} alone. A near-1 \rho_{\max} along a direction u_3 costs almost nothing if \hat u_{\text{claim}} loads mostly on u_1 with s_1 large. This is why §5.1 reports the full spectrum \{\rho_k\} together with the projection c_k, and why the decision is made on the numerical value \lambda(A_{\text{claim}}^{\text{aud}}, \hat u_{\text{claim}}), not on a scalar threshold \rho^\ast.

