Appendix E — Boundary effects from \sigma_{\text{int}} \ge 0

The purpose of this appendix is to identify precisely where non-standard asymptotics do arise in the model, and to give the honest justification for the Monte Carlo calibration in §5.3.

E.1 The source of the boundary

The hierarchical prior on \sigma_{\text{int},j} requires \sigma_{\text{int},j} \ge 0. Let \Sigma(\sigma_{\text{int}}) = \operatorname{diag}(\sigma_i^2 + \sigma_{\text{int}, \text{instr}(i)}^2) and W(\sigma_{\text{int}}) = \Sigma(\sigma_{\text{int}})^{-1}. The profiled statistic is

T(\hat\sigma_{\text{int}}) = \hat d(\hat\sigma_{\text{int}})^\top S(\hat\sigma_{\text{int}})\, \hat d(\hat\sigma_{\text{int}}),

where \hat d(\sigma_{\text{int}}) and S(\sigma_{\text{int}}) are the d-estimator and profiled information at fixed \sigma_{\text{int}}, and \hat\sigma_{\text{int}} is the estimate of the scatter.

The v0.6 text claims: "the per-instrument intrinsic scatter sits on a boundary at zero; the estimated scatter, small samples, and the informative prior in variant (b) can distort the tail." The following propositions give the precise content.

E.2 The scatter test itself is a boundary problem

If one tests H_0^{\text{scat}}: \sigma_{\text{int}} = 0 against H_1^{\text{scat}}: \sigma_{\text{int}} > 0, the LRT statistic is (Self & Liang 1987, Case 4; Chernoff 1954)

-2\log\Lambda_{\text{scat}} \;\xrightarrow{\;d\;}\; \tfrac12 \chi^2_0 + \tfrac12\chi^2_1,

for a single instrument. For k instruments with independent scatter parameters on the boundary at zero,

-2\log\Lambda_{\text{scat}} \;\xrightarrow{\;d\;}\; \sum_{j=0}^{k} 2^{-k}\binom{k}{j}\, \chi^2_j,

with the \chi^2_0 component interpreted as a point mass at zero. This is the only place the boundary-null apparatus of Chernoff/Self–Liang/Shapiro is mathematically appropriate in this thesis, and §13 accordingly cites those references only for the scatter-test discussion.

E.3 Nuisance-boundary effects on the dipole test

The dipole test is a different object. Here \sigma_{\text{int}} is a nuisance, not the parameter of interest. The relevant question is: does estimating \sigma_{\text{int}} at or near its boundary distort the null distribution of T = \hat d^\top S \hat d?

Proposition E.1 (Nuisance-boundary perturbation). Let \sigma_{\text{int}}^\star denote the true scatter, \hat\sigma_{\text{int}} the estimate, and T(\sigma_{\text{int}}^\star) the LRT statistic evaluated at the true variance. Then

T(\hat\sigma_{\text{int}}) - T(\sigma_{\text{int}}^\star)
\;=\; \nabla_{\sigma_{\text{int}}} T(\sigma_{\text{int}}^\star)^\top (\hat\sigma_{\text{int}} - \sigma_{\text{int}}^\star) + O_p(\|\hat\sigma_{\text{int}} - \sigma_{\text{int}}^\star\|^2).

If \sigma_{\text{int}}^\star > 0 (interior), \sqrt{n}(\hat\sigma_{\text{int}} - \sigma_{\text{int}}^\star) = O_p(1), and the perturbation is O_p(n^{-1/2}) — asymptotically negligible, and T(\hat\sigma_{\text{int}}) \xrightarrow{d} \chi^2_3 by Slutsky.

If \sigma_{\text{int}}^\star = 0 (boundary), the situation is qualitatively different: \hat\sigma_{\text{int}} \ge 0 is a one-sided estimator whose distribution under the null is a mixture with a point mass at zero. The perturbation term \nabla_{\sigma_{\text{int}}} T \cdot (\hat\sigma_{\text{int}} - 0) \cdot \mathbf{1}\{\hat\sigma_{\text{int}} > 0\} has non-vanishing variance at the boundary. The \chi^2_3 reference is then not the asymptotic distribution of T(\hat\sigma_{\text{int}}); the correct distribution is a mixture that depends on the design and the true \sigma_i.

Sketch. The MLE at a boundary is the positive-part estimator; its distribution is a mixture with mass P_0 := \Pr(\hat\sigma_{\text{int}} = 0) > 0 at the boundary point. The map \sigma_{\text{int}} \mapsto T(\sigma_{\text{int}}) is smooth in a neighbourhood of \sigma_{\text{int}}^\star = 0 (the design and weights are smooth functions of the variance), so Taylor expansion applies conditional on \hat\sigma_{\text{int}} > 0, but the mixture weight P_0 concentrates mass at T(0), not at \chi^2_3. The result is not a chi-squared mixture in T (the T-map is nonlinear in \sigma_{\text{int}}), but it is not \chi^2_3 either. \square

Consequence for the protocol. The v0.6 claim (§5.3) is that the Monte Carlo calibration quantifies departures from \chi^2_3 — it is a validation, not a necessity. Proposition E.1 makes this precise: the departure is second-order when \sigma_{\text{int}}^\star > 0 and well estimated, and material when \sigma_{\text{int}}^\star \approx 0 or \hat\sigma_{\text{int}} is small relative to the \sigma_i. The Year-1 deliverable in §10 is to quantify the departure as a function of (\sigma_{\text{int}}^\star, \bar\sigma, n, k) and to use the empirical null in the decision rule if the departure exceeds the pre-specified tolerance (i.e., if the \chi^2_3 critical value differs from the Monte Carlo empirical critical value by more than a pre-registered amount).

E.4 Informative priors in variants (b) and (c)

For variant (b), \delta has a Gaussian prior with covariance \Sigma_{\delta,\text{archive}}. The posterior mean and covariance are

\hat d_{(b)} = (X_d^\top \widetilde W X_d)^{-1}X_d^\top \widetilde W y, \qquad \widetilde W = \Sigma^{-1} - \Sigma^{-1}X_\Delta(\Sigma_{\delta,\text{archive}}^{-1} + X_\Delta^\top \Sigma^{-1}X_\Delta)^{-1}X_\Delta^\top \Sigma^{-1}.

The frequentist null distribution of T_{(b)} := \hat d_{(b)}^\top S_{(b)}\hat d_{(b)} is not \chi^2_3 exactly — the prior on \delta is not a frequentist constraint, so the "estimate" \hat d_{(b)} is biased toward zero unless the prior is centred at the truth. The bias is O(\|X_d^\top\widetilde WX_\Delta\, \delta_\star\|), and the variance is dominated by the "effective information" S_{(b)} = X_d^\top \widetilde WX_d.

This is why variant (b) requires Monte Carlo calibration as a first-class method, not as a check. The frequentist reference distribution for variant (b) has no closed form. The Monte Carlo under the fitted model produces the reference. The pre-specified procedure in §5.4 (Clopper–Pearson with target half-width) applies to variant (b) as to the others.

