Appendix F — Monte Carlo precision and the binomial framework

F.1 Clopper–Pearson exact interval

Let X \sim \mathrm{Bin}(N, p) with unknown p and observed X = x. The 100(1-\gamma)\% Clopper–Pearson confidence interval for p is

\big[\, \mathrm{Beta}^{-1}(\gamma/2;\, x,\, N - x + 1),\;\; \mathrm{Beta}^{-1}(1 - \gamma/2;\, x + 1,\, N - x) \,\big],

where \mathrm{Beta}^{-1}(q; a, b) is the q-quantile of the \mathrm{Beta}(a,b) distribution. The interval has coverage \ge 1 - \gamma for all p \in [0,1] (conservative, no large-N approximation).

F.2 Zero-failure bound

For x = 0 and large N,

\mathrm{Beta}^{-1}(1 - \gamma/2;\, 1,\, N) \approx -\ln(\gamma/2)/N.

For \gamma = 0.05, -\ln(0.025) \approx 3.689, so the upper bound is approximately 3.69/N. Hence a zero-failure 95% upper bound below \alpha requires

N > -\ln(\gamma/2)/\alpha \approx 3.69/\alpha.

For \alpha = 1.35 \times 10^{-3}, N \gtrsim 2{,}733. This is the exact version of the rule-of-three figure quoted in §5.4.

F.3 Target half-width framework

Let [L_N(x), U_N(x)] be the Clopper–Pearson interval. The pre-specified precision target is

U_N(x) - L_N(x) \;\le\; 2h,

with h a number stated before freeze (e.g., h = 5 \times 10^{-4}). For a given anticipated FPR \hat p \approx \alpha, the required N is

N_{\min}(\hat p, h) = \min\{N : U_N(\lfloor N \hat p \rceil) - L_N(\lfloor N \hat p \rceil) \le 2h\}.

Numerical remark. For \hat p = 1.35 \times 10^{-3} and h = 5 \times 10^{-4} (a 37% relative half-width), the Wald approximation gives N \approx \hat p (1-\hat p)/h^2 \approx 5{,}400; the exact Clopper–Pearson requirement is conservative, typically a factor of 1.5–2 larger, giving N \approx 8{,}000–11{,}000. For h = 10^{-4} (a 7.4% relative half-width), N scales as h^{-2}, giving N \approx 1.3 \times 10^6 — this is the "compute budget" issue flagged in §9 and §10.

The v0.4 rule-of-three figure (N \approx 2{,}223) is a zero-failure minimum. It is not the precision-target minimum. The protocol's precision target is the binding constraint and must be stated before freeze.

F.4 Detecting a factor-of-two inflation

For the held-out consistency check (Appendix H), the requirement is: given a held-out sample of size N_{\text{held}} and a true FPR of 2\alpha, reject the null hypothesis p = \alpha with probability at least 1 - \beta_{\text{held}} = 0.80.

Let X \sim \mathrm{Bin}(N_{\text{held}}, 2\alpha) under the alternative. The Wald (large-N) requirement is

2\alpha - z_{1-\beta_{\text{held}}}\sqrt{2\alpha(1 - 2\alpha)/N_{\text{held}}}
\;>\;
\alpha + z_{1-\gamma/2}\sqrt{\alpha(1-\alpha)/N_{\text{held}}},

which, for \alpha \ll 1, reduces to

N_{\text{held}} \;\gtrsim\; \frac{(z_{1-\gamma/2} + z_{1-\beta_{\text{held}}}\sqrt{2})^2}{\alpha}.

For \gamma = 0.05, \beta_{\text{held}} = 0.20: (1.96 + 0.84 \cdot 1.414)^2 \approx (1.96 + 1.19)^2 \approx 9.92, so

N_{\text{held}} \gtrsim 9.92/\alpha \approx 7{,}350 \text{ for } \alpha = 1.35 \times 10^{-3}.

The exact Clopper–Pearson calculation is more conservative; expect N_{\text{held}} \approx 10^4 per variant. This is the number the Y1 computational feasibility study must confirm (or, if compute is binding, the target factor-of-two inflation must be relaxed to a factor of three, with the corresponding N reduced to \approx 3 \times 10^3).

