Appendix G — Bayes factor calibration as a classifier

G.1 The Bayes factor for the Gaussian linear model

Let M_2 be the model with \eta only and M_3 the model with (d, \eta). Under M_3, assign a Gaussian prior d \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \tau^2 I_3). Under a flat (or fixed) prior on \eta, the Bayes factor is (conditional on the fixed \eta at its MLE; the marginalisation over \eta introduces a correction that is second-order in n^{-1} and is included in the numerical implementation but omitted from the analytic display):

BF_{32} \;=\; \frac{p(y \mid M_3)}{p(y \mid M_2)} \;=\; \int_{\mathbb{R}^3} \frac{L(d, \hat\eta_3)}{L(0, \hat\eta_2)} \, \pi(d)\, dd.

The likelihood ratio L(d)/L(0) under the Gaussian model is

\frac{L(d)}{L(0)} \;=\; \exp\!\Big(-\tfrac12 (d - \hat d)^\top S (d - \hat d) + \tfrac12 \hat d^\top S\hat d\Big)
\;=\; \exp\!\Big(-\tfrac12 d^\top S d + d^\top S\hat d\Big).

G.2 Closed-form integral

Theorem G.1. Under d \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \tau^2 I_3),

BF_{32} \;=\; |I_3 + \tau^2 S|^{-1/2}\, \exp\!\Big(\tfrac12\tau^4 (S\hat d)^\top (I_3 + \tau^2 S)^{-1}(S\hat d)\Big).

Proof. Let d = \tau u with u \sim \mathcal{N}(0, I_3). Then

BF_{32} = E_u\big[\exp(-\tfrac12\tau^2 u^\top S u + \tau u^\top S\hat d)\big].

This is the moment-generating function of a quadratic form evaluated at a point, for which the standard formula is (Magnus 1978, Theorem 2):

E_u\big[\exp(x^\top u - \tfrac12 u^\top K u)\big] = |I + K|^{-1/2}\exp\!\big(\tfrac12 x^\top (I+K)^{-1} x\big),

with K = \tau^2 S and x = \tau S \hat d. Substituting:

BF_{32} = |I_3 + \tau^2 S|^{-1/2} \exp\!\big(\tfrac12 \tau^2 (S\hat d)^\top (I_3 + \tau^2 S)^{-1} (S\hat d)\big).

The exponent simplifies: \tau^2 \cdot \tau^2 = \tau^4, giving the stated expression. \square

Corollary G.2 (Spectral form). Let S = U\Lambda U^\top with \Lambda = \operatorname{diag}(s_1, s_2, s_3) and u_k = U e_k. Then

\log BF_{32} \;=\; -\tfrac12 \sum_{k=1}^3 \log(1 + \tau^2 s_k) \;+\; \tfrac12 \tau^4 \sum_{k=1}^3 \frac{s_k^2 (u_k^\top \hat d)^2}{1 + \tau^2 s_k}.

G.3 Null distribution of the Bayes factor

Under H_0 (d=0), \hat d \sim \mathcal{N}(0, S^{-1}). Let Z_k := \sqrt{s_k}\, u_k^\top \hat d. Then Z_k \stackrel{\text{iid}}{\sim} \mathcal{N}(0,1), and

\log BF_{32} \;\stackrel{H_0}{=}\; -\tfrac12 \sum_k \log(1 + \tau^2 s_k) \;+\; \tfrac12 \sum_k \frac{\tau^4 s_k}{1 + \tau^2 s_k}\, Z_k^2.

This is a weighted sum of independent \chi^2_1 variables. The null distribution of BF_{32} is therefore a generalized chi-squared distribution. Its quantiles are computed by Imhof's (1961) numerical method, or by Monte Carlo; either is fine. The exact null distribution does depend on the design (through the s_k) and on the prior scale \tau.

G.4 Calibration as a classifier

The classifier's operating characteristics are:

· FPR: \alpha_{BF}(\tau, c) = \Pr_{H_0}(BF_{32} > c \mid \tau).
· Power: \pi_{BF}(\tau, c, d_\star) = \Pr_{H_1(d_\star)}(BF_{32} > c \mid \tau).

The decision rule uses the anchored scale \tau = \tau_{\text{dec}} (matched to A_{\text{claim}}^{\text{aud}}) and the threshold c chosen on the calibration set so that \alpha_{BF}(\tau_{\text{dec}}, c) \le \alpha with equality at the boundary. The power curve is measured on the held-out set. The diagnostic scale \tau = \tau_{\text{diag}} (broad) is reported but not used in the decision.

Proposition G.3. If the threshold c is chosen by calibration, the Bayes factor classifier has FPR exactly \alpha by construction (up to Clopper–Pearson uncertainty on the calibration set), and the BF is therefore a valid decision rule in the frequentist sense — even though it is not a prior-neutral evidence measure.

This is the content of §5.7's claim that "the Bayes factor is a classifier, not an unbiased summary of evidence."

