Appendix H — Held-out validation and the consistency criterion

H.1 The formal criterion

Let \hat\alpha_{\text{held}} = X_{\text{held}}/N_{\text{held}} be the held-out FPR estimate. The consistency criterion is

\alpha \;\in\; [L_{N_{\text{held}}}(\hat\alpha_{\text{held}}),\, U_{N_{\text{held}}}(\hat\alpha_{\text{held}})],

with L, U the Clopper–Pearson bounds. This is a two-sided criterion with confidence level 1-\gamma, and it is pre-specified before unblinding.

H.2 Sample size

Given the target alternative \alpha_{\text{alt}} = 2\alpha (factor-of-two inflation), the required N_{\text{held}} for 80% power to reject the null is derived in Appendix F.4: N_{\text{held}} \gtrsim 9.92/\alpha \approx 7{,}350 for \alpha = 1.35 \times 10^{-3}, and the exact value is computed numerically in the Y1 feasibility study.

H.3 Interpretation

The consistency criterion tests one specific failure mode: pipeline overfitting that inflates the FPR. It does not test:

· Model misspecification. If the fitted null model is wrong, both calibration and held-out simulations are wrong in the same way, and the held-out criterion passes by construction. The negative controls (S3, Appendix E.3) probe this gap, but they do not close it.
· Nuisance-boundary distortions. If \sigma_{\text{int}} is near zero (Appendix E.3), the held-out simulations under the fitted \hat\sigma_{\text{int}} inherit the same boundary behaviour, so the held-out criterion does not detect this distortion. The Year-1 χ²₃-adequacy study is the appropriate check.

This is the content of §5.6's caveat that "the held-out FPR is not over-interpreted as evidence that the fitted null model is correct."

