Appendix I — Track B: Poisson binned likelihood and profile bound

I.1 The likelihood

Let \{E_j\}_{j=1}^{J} be the bin boundaries of the published UHECR spectrum, and n_j the observed count in bin j. The Poisson likelihood is

L(b^{-1}, \boldsymbol\theta_{\text{prop}}, \eta \mid \{n_j\}) \;=\; \prod_{j=1}^{J} \frac{\mu_j(b^{-1}, \boldsymbol\theta_{\text{prop}}, \eta)^{n_j} e^{-\mu_j(b^{-1}, \boldsymbol\theta_{\text{prop}}, \eta)}}{n_j!},

where

\mu_j(b^{-1}, \boldsymbol\theta_{\text{prop}}, \eta) \;=\; \int_{E_j}^{E_{j+1}} A_{\text{eff}}(E)\, \Phi(E; b^{-1}, \boldsymbol\theta_{\text{prop}}, \eta)\, dE,

with A_{\text{eff}} the detector effective area (known from the published instrument description), \Phi the predicted differential flux under the lattice-cutoff hypothesis (following Beane et al. 2014), and \eta an energy-scale nuisance parameter defined by E_{\text{true}} = \eta\, E_{\text{reco}}.

I.2 Profile likelihood and the confidence bound

Define the profile likelihood

L_{\text{prof}}(b^{-1}) \;=\; \max_{\boldsymbol\theta_{\text{prop}}, \eta} L(b^{-1}, \boldsymbol\theta_{\text{prop}}, \eta).

The 100(1-\gamma)\% confidence set for b^{-1} is

\mathcal{C}_\gamma \;=\; \big\{ b^{-1} > 0 : -2\log\big(L_{\text{prof}}(b^{-1})/L_{\text{prof}}(\hat b^{-1})\big) \le \chi^2_{1, 1-\gamma} \big\},

with \hat b^{-1} = \arg\max_{b^{-1}} L_{\text{prof}}(b^{-1}). The reported bound is b^{-1}_{\text{lower}} = \inf \mathcal{C}_\gamma.

Proposition I.1 (Exact null reference). Under the null hypothesis that the cosmic-ray cutoff is not a lattice cutoff (i.e., the true b^{-1} is larger than the observed cutoff, so the lattice plays no role at observable energies), the profile-likelihood ratio statistic above is asymptotically \chi^2_1. This is the standard Wilks result for a single parameter in the interior of the parameter space. No boundary-null correction is required.

Proposition I.2 (Vacuous-bound condition). The confidence set \mathcal{C}_\gamma is vacuous (extends to b^{-1} = 0) iff the profile likelihood is flat over b^{-1} \in (0, b^{-1}_{\max}] at level \gamma. This occurs when the lattice cutoff is degenerate with the source maximum energy E_{\max} or with propagation losses. The condition is detected by comparing the profile curvature to the \chi^2_1 threshold, and the result is reported as "unconstrained at the current sensitivity" — a valid outcome, not a failure.

I.3 Energy-scale nuisance

The energy-scale nuisance \eta is constrained by the instrument's calibration uncertainty. Following the published instrument papers, the prior is \eta \sim \mathcal{N}(1, \sigma_\eta^2) with \sigma_\eta the fractional energy-scale uncertainty (typically 5–20% for UHECR detectors). The nuisance is profiled in the likelihood, so the reported bound is robust to energy-scale mis-calibration up to the stated \sigma_\eta. If the bound is dominated by \eta rather than by b^{-1}, this is reported explicitly.

I.4 Assumption audit

The Beane et al. (2014) derivation assumes:

1. The observed cosmic-ray cutoff energy is a lattice cutoff, not a propagation effect.
2. The lattice spacing is related to the cutoff by E_{\text{cutoff}} \sim \pi/b (Brillouin-zone argument).
3. The lattice isotropy is preserved at observable energies (no rotational-symmetry-breaking signature at current sensitivity).

The audit tests (1) by computing the profile likelihood comparison between the lattice-cutoff model and a conventional propagation-cutoff model (e.g., photodisintegration on the CMB). Assumption (2) is a theoretical input from Beane et al. and is adopted. Assumption (3) is out of scope for the current constraint — it is a future-sensitivity target (§6, deliverable 3).

