Appendix J — The decision partition: formal statement

J.1 The diagnostic space

Let \mathcal{D} = \mathcal{G}_0 \times \mathcal{P} \times \mathcal{B} \times \mathcal{T}, where:

· \mathcal{G}_0 \in \{\text{pass}, \text{fail}\},
· \mathcal{P} = (\lambda, \{\rho_k\}, \text{power}, \alpha_{\text{empirical}}) is the power/identifiability sector, with \lambda \in \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}, \{\rho_k\} \in [0,1]^3, power \in [0,1],
· \mathcal{B} = (T_{\text{key}}, BF, T_{\text{hist}}, T_{\text{off}}) is the substantive-test sector,
· \mathcal{T} is the prior-sensitivity sector (\mathcal{T} = 1 if variants (b) and (c) classify differently, else 0).

J.2 The regions

The partition \{\mathcal{R}_1, \ldots, \mathcal{R}_7\} is defined by:

\begin{aligned}
\mathcal{R}_{\text{infeasible}} &: \mathcal{G}_0 = \text{fail}, \\
\mathcal{R}_{\text{underpowered}} &: \mathcal{G}_0 = \text{pass} \;\wedge\; \lambda < \lambda_{\min} \;\wedge\; \text{(any } T \text{ value)}, \\
\mathcal{R}_{\text{prior-sensitive}} &: \mathcal{G}_0 = \text{pass} \;\wedge\; \lambda \ge \lambda_{\min} \;\wedge\; \mathcal{T} = 1, \\
\mathcal{R}_{\text{survives}} &: \mathcal{G}_0 = \text{pass} \;\wedge\; \lambda \ge \lambda_{\min} \;\wedge\; \mathcal{T} = 0 \;\wedge\; p_{\text{key}} < \alpha \;\wedge\; BF > c_\alpha, \\
\mathcal{R}_{\text{not-robust}} &: \mathcal{G}_0 = \text{pass} \;\wedge\; \lambda \ge \lambda_{\min} \;\wedge\; \mathcal{T} = 0 \;\wedge\; p_{\text{key}} \ge \alpha \;\wedge\; p_{\text{hist}} < \alpha \;\wedge\; p_{\text{off}} < \alpha, \\
\mathcal{R}_{\text{hist-null}} &: \mathcal{G}_0 = \text{pass} \;\wedge\; \lambda \ge \lambda_{\min} \;\wedge\; \mathcal{T} = 0 \;\wedge\; p_{\text{key}} \ge \alpha \;\wedge\; p_{\text{hist}} \ge \alpha, \\
\mathcal{R}_{\text{discordant}} &: \text{the complement of the union of the above.}
\end{aligned}

J.3 Totality and disjointness

Proposition J.1. The seven regions \{\mathcal{R}_{\text{infeasible}}, \mathcal{R}_{\text{underpowered}}, \mathcal{R}_{\text{prior-sensitive}}, \mathcal{R}_{\text{survives}}, \mathcal{R}_{\text{not-robust}}, \mathcal{R}_{\text{hist-null}}, \mathcal{R}_{\text{discordant}}\} partition \mathcal{D}.

Proof. Each region is defined by a conjunction of mutually exclusive conditions on the elements of \mathcal{D}. The infeasible/underpowered/prior-sensitive/survives/not-robust/hist-null regions are sequentially exclusive (once \mathcal{G}_0, \lambda, and \mathcal{T} are fixed, the remaining conditions partition into disjoint quadrants of (p_{\text{key}}, p_{\text{hist}}, p_{\text{off}}, BF)). The discordant region is defined as the complement; the union is all of \mathcal{D}, and the regions are pairwise disjoint by construction. \square

Proposition J.2 (Precedence is well-defined). The classification of any D \in \mathcal{D} is unique.

Proof. Immediate from Proposition J.1 (partition implies unique membership). Note that the precedence in the prose of §5.8 is the algorithmic realization of the partition; the partition itself does not require an ordering, it is a set-theoretic object. The prose order is chosen so that the first-matched region is the classification, which is well-defined because the regions are disjoint. \square

J.4 Why the partition is not the v0.4 prose

The v0.4 decision rule was a precedence-ordered list of conditions with prose-described boundaries. The v0.6 rule is a partition of the diagnostic space with boundaries fixed by the simulation study before freeze. The difference is operational:

· In v0.4, a dataset with \lambda < \lambda_{\min} and p_{\text{key}} < \alpha could arguably be classified as "survives" by a careless reader, because the list did not enforce disjointness.
· In v0.6, \mathcal{R}_{\text{underpowered}} is defined to include all points with \lambda < \lambda_{\min}, so the classification is unambiguous.

This is the mathematical content of §5.8's claim that "the boundary between 'underpowered' and 'discordant' is not left to judgment."

J.5 Numerical determination of the boundaries

The only non-trivial boundary is the value of \lambda_{\min}, which depends on (\alpha, 1-\beta) through the non-central \chi^2_3 distribution (Appendix D.2). For \alpha = 1.35 \times 10^{-3} and 1-\beta = 0.80, \lambda_{\min} is a fixed number — it is not estimated from the data and does not depend on the design. This makes the boundary of \mathcal{R}_{\text{underpowered}} a pre-computed constant.

The BF threshold c_\alpha is calibrated on the calibration set (Appendix G.4) and is fixed before unblinding. Its uncertainty is reported as a range, with the pre-registered decision rule using the point estimate and a sensitivity check using the range endpoints.

The scatter-boundary corrections (Appendix E.3) enter through the empirical null distribution of T, which may replace the \chi^2_3 critical value in the computation of p_{\text{key}}. This is the only data-dependent piece, and it is fixed before unblinding.

