Appendix K — Reference invariance

Proposition K.1. The statistics T_{\text{key}}, T_{\text{hist}}, T_{\text{off}}, BF_{32}, and \{\rho_k\} are invariant to the choice of reference instrument.

Proof. Changing the reference instrument from r to r' sends \delta_j \mapsto \delta_j - \delta_{r'} for all j. This is a linear reparametrization of the \eta-block: \eta \mapsto \eta + c \mathbf{1} for a constant shift c = -\delta_{r'} applied to the \delta components, with the intercept B absorbing the constant (Appendix A.1, F2). The W-projector P_\eta and the residual operator M_\eta are invariant to such reparametrizations, hence so is every statistic constructed from M_\eta and y. \square

Corollary K.2. The individual \delta_j are not physical parameters; only differences \delta_j - \delta_{j'} are invariant. This is why §5.2 refers to \delta_{\text{ref}} = 0 as a convention rather than a physical constraint.

